.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Vfs
is the kernel's own client of 9P2000.L: everything above it names files with
paths, and everything below it names them with fids.
There is no global tree.
.I Resolve
starts at a
.B Namespace 's
root and asks each server in turn, consulting the mount table between every
element, so two processes can each have a
.B /dev/mouse
and the two can be different files.
.SS Server
A
.B Server
pairs a 9P handler with the session a caller reaches it through.
It sits on one of two transports and does not know which:
.I server_init
wires a handler onto
.BR vectra9.In_Process ,
where the handler runs on the caller's own stack with no queue and no copy.
.I Server_start
moves it onto
.IR mnt (9)
instead, with
.I workers
threads answering from a queue, several requests in flight, and an
.B abort
hook a caller's deadline can reach through
.BR Tflush .
.I Server_stop
reverses the move.
A chan that survives it still names a fid the handler knows.
A handler must be safe under several threads at once and must build any
payload it answers with in the buffer it is handed.
It need not know or care which transport it is on.
.PP
A
.B Server
is released by count, not by a single owner: every
.B Chan
that names it holds one count and every non-chan stake
(a
.B /srv
name, a mount in progress) holds another.
When both reach zero, the
.B release
hook set at construction runs once, on the thread that dropped the last
one.
.PP
.B #name
device servers are a small, fixed table of at most
.B MAX_DEVICES
(16), filled once at boot by
.I register_device
and read by every
.B #
path a caller resolves.
Duplicate names are refused, not shadowed.
.SS Chan
A
.B Chan
is a handle on a file in a namespace, as distinct from a fid, which is a
handle on a file in one session.
It carries the server, the fid, the qid, and two fields that make
.B ..
work across a mount:
.B tree_root
(the qid this chan's tree is rooted at) and
.B mounted_over
(the chan that tree was mounted onto).
Chans are reference counted.
.I Chan_clone
costs a message (a
.B Twalk
with no names, 9P's way to get a second handle).
It is for a copy that will be opened or walked independently.
.I Chan_incref
costs nothing.
It is for a copy that will not.
.I Chan_close
drops a reference and, on the last one, clunks the fid and walks the
.B mounted_over
chain, releasing each parent in turn.
.PP
.I Chan_read
and
.I chan_write
move at most one message's worth.
A caller loops, in chunks by
.IR chan_iounit ,
the server's own promise from
.B Rlopen
folded against the session's msize.
.I Chan_read_for
is a read with a deadline, the reason this package sits on
.IR mnt (9)
at all: it flushes and returns
.B EINTR
rather than leave a caller parked past its patience.
.SS Namespace
A
.B Namespace
is a root chan plus a mount table, and nothing else -- no current
directory, no global tree.
.I Resolve
takes only absolute paths and
.B #name
specs.
A relative path needs a process to be relative to, and that
resolution lives in
.IR user (9).
.PP
.I Ns_fork
gives a child its own namespace, following
.IR fork (2)'s
.B Fork_Flag
bit set.
.B Share
(the default, no flag) hands back the same pointer, so a later
.I bind
in either process is visible to both.
.B Copy
duplicates the mount table and shares the member chans, so the two
namespaces start identical and then diverge independently.
.B Clean
starts with no root and no mounts.
.B Clean
with
.B Copy
is
.BR Clean .
A namespace's own
.B lock
is a sleeping read/write lock: a lookup reads it, and a
.I bind
or
.I unmount
writes it and waits for every lookup in flight.
.SS The mount table
A mount point is keyed by
.RI ( "server identity" ", " qid.path ),
never by the whole qid, because
.B qid.version
changes on every write to the directory and keying on it would unmount a
tree the moment something wrote into what it covers.
Each key holds an ordered list of
.B Mount
members, searched in list order, which is what a union directory is:
.I bind
with
.RI ( Mount_Order )
.B Before
pushes a new member to the front (searched first),
.B After
to the back (searched last), and
.B Replace
clears the list and becomes its only member.
A member carries the
.B Create
flag to mark where new files are made.
At most one member should carry it, and
.I union_create_target
answers nil, not a fallback, when none does -- a create that lands
somewhere the namespace did not choose is a bug the caller must see.
.PP
.I Bind_path
and
.I mount_device
resolve their two path arguments and call
.IR bind .
The difference is only where the source comes from, a name already in the
namespace or a
.B #name
device.
.I Unmount
removes one member by
.RI ( "server, qid.path" )
identity, or the whole mount point when its
.B source
chan is nil.
It dissolves the mount point rather than freeing it, because
a chan reached through a union keeps a reference to it through
.BR Chan.union_head .
A member's
.B id
is a monotonic counter local to its mount point, assigned at
.I bind
and never reused.
It is the cookie a union
.I readdir
resumes by, so a member added or removed between two reads does not shift
the ones after it.
.SS Walking
Resolving one path element is two steps.
.I Walk1
asks each member of a union in turn for the name, through
.BR Twalk .
.I Cross_mounts
then checks whether the file just reached is itself a mount point and, if
so, substitutes the first member of what is mounted there.
A single path element can therefore cross from one server to another, and
a full path can involve as many servers as it has mount points.
.I Resolve
walks a whole path this way, closing every intermediate chan as it moves
past it, so a failed resolve leaks nothing.
.I Resolve_mount_point
is the same walk with the last crossing withheld, which is the chan
.I bind
and
.I unmount
need: the key a mount is filed under, not what is mounted there.
.PP
.B ..
out of the root of a mounted tree cannot be answered by the server that
root belongs to -- it does not know it was mounted, or where.
.I Walk_up
climbs the
.B mounted_over
chain until it stands somewhere with a parent its own server can name,
which may cross several stacked mounts at once, then asks that server for
.BR .. .
At the namespace root,
.B ..
is the root: there is nowhere to climb to, which is what makes a
chroot-equivalent free.
.PP
.I Open_path
is
.I resolve
then
.IR chan_open .
.I Create_path
splits a path at its last slash, resolves the parent, and asks the server
there to create the name.
What a create under a union lands in is decided
by
.I union_create_target
during the walk, the same as an open.
.SS Fid_Table
.RB ( fidtab.odin )
is one fid table a server binds a client's fid to a small integer node
index with.
It exists so a second server does not mean a second implementation of the
map: one allocation, chained free list, no lock of its own -- a fid table
is server state, and the server already has a lock for the rest of its
state.
9P's two rules about an open fid are the caller's to keep: a walk on an
already-open fid is refused, and a read before
.B Tlopen
is refused, both as
.B EBUSY
or
.B EINVAL
at the handler that checks
.B Fid_Slot.open
before it acts.
.SS Static_Tree
.RB ( static.odin )
is a read-only server over a fixed table of
.BR Static_Node ,
each a name, a parent index, and either a directory flag or file data
living in
.BR .rodata .
Its handler never allocates and a read costs no copy: an
.B Rread
points straight at the node's string.
.IR root (3)
and
.IR bin (3)
are each one
.B Static_Tree
of their own.
.SS Locks
.RB ( lock.odin )
The rule that governs every other one:
.B "no spinlock in this package is ever held across a 9P message."
A spinlock is the interrupt flag.
A message can park the sending thread,
and on
.IR mnt (9)
it always does.
.I Rpc
checks this rather than trusts it --
.I rpc_ready
refuses with
.B EDEADLK
when a spinlock is held anywhere on the CPU, not only one of this
package's own.
.PP
Two kinds of lock, never confused.
A
.B sync.Spinlock
guards a pointer or a count for a handful of instructions and is never
held across a message.
A
.B sync.RW_Lock
sleeps and is held across one on purpose, because a union search reads a
mount point for the whole of its search and a
.I bind
must wait for every search in flight rather than race it.
Lock order is fixed:
.B Namespace.lock
before
.B Mount_Point.lock
before
.BR object_lock .
A read lock on one mount point is never held while a second is taken.
.B object_lock
is one global spinlock over every
.B Chan.refs ,
.B Mount_Point.refs
and
.B Namespace.refs ,
because those objects outlive the namespace that created them whenever
another namespace still references them through
.B ns_fork
or
.BR Chan.union_head .
.B device_lock
guards the
.B #name
table alone.
.PP
A
.B Chan
itself is not internally synchronised for
.IR chan_open ,
.IR chan_read
or
.IR chan_write .
Whoever holds a reference owns it, exactly like a descriptor.
Two threads sharing one handle have an arrangement of their own to keep.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B #/
the root server, an ordinary
.B Static_Tree
with no special casing in the walker
.RB ( root.odin ).
